# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Pipe Eleven Moves, Three Stay Open

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 113 BPM and begins with Seoyeon holding a four-note saenghwang cluster while Kabelo leaves the first eight bars almost empty: soft shaker, one compact kick, and a single low log-drum note every second bar. Three saenghwang voices remain fixed. The fourth, identified on the score as pipe eleven, moves down one scale degree at the end of bar eight. Only then does Kabelo answer with a three-note descending log-drum cell.

Jiwoo confirms the new pitch with one damped yanggeum fifth. Donghyun replaces the next programmed rim with a dry chae-side janggu stroke. Nomsa waits until Seoyeon removes one of the fixed tones, then enters with a breath-heavy bass-flute fourth through the newly opened frequency window.

At the midpoint, Seoyeon moves pipe eleven upward instead of downward. Kabelo mirrors the reversal with an ascending log-drum answer, but keeps every other drum unchanged. The final section holds the same chord for sixteen bars without any substitution, so the expected log-drum response never comes. The track ends on sustained reeds and shaker alone.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary image of a saenghwang with one bamboo pipe marked 11 on a paper inspection diagram, three straight horizontal pitch lines and one moving downward, yellow customs floor stripe and a small log-drum waveform display nearby. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “eleven moves,” “three stay open,” “answer below,” and “hold the breath.”

## Track 2: Yellow Line Holds the Chord, Log Drum Crosses

**Story & Context:** The second track runs at 116 BPM with the EP’s most active low end. Seoyeon sustains a three-note suspended cluster for thirty-two bars while changing only the highest tone every four bars. Kabelo writes log-drum phrases that repeatedly cross below and above the perceived root through octave jumps and short pitch glides, but never overlap the exact moment the saenghwang voice changes.

Jiwoo uses two prepared yanggeum notes a minor second apart, striking one or the other depending on which upper pipe Seoyeon selects. Donghyun alternates a muted janggu bass stroke with a removed electronic kick, creating a breathing low-percussion pattern. Nomsa plays only key clicks for the first half before entering with a low sustained note when the saenghwang drops from four sounding pipes to two.

In the last minute, Kabelo removes the kick entirely while leaving the log drum intact. The track remains danceable because the low melodic percussion and janggu imply the missing pulse. The final chord substitution occurs with no log answer, leaving Jiwoo’s short metallic note as the only confirmation.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Long yellow customs floor line crossing beneath a hand-drawn chord lattice while a curved log-drum waveform passes from one side to the other, prepared yanggeum strings and saenghwang pipes in shallow focus. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “hold the line,” “cross underneath,” “upper pipe changed,” and “leave the kick out.”

## Track 3: Breath Clears Before the Last Low Note

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 110 BPM with Seoyeon playing the thinnest voicing on the EP: two saenghwang notes held in an open fifth. Kabelo uses sparse shaker, one kick, and isolated log-drum roots. Every eight bars Seoyeon adds one new inner voice, gradually thickening the chord lattice without increasing volume.

Jiwoo answers only the newly added tones with single prepared-yanggeum strikes. Donghyun progressively removes programmed percussion positions and replaces them with quieter janggu finger taps. Nomsa enters after the third added voice with a low bass-flute fundamental, then withdraws when the saenghwang reaches its fullest cluster.

In the final passage, the process reverses. Seoyeon removes one pipe at a time while sustaining breath across the changes. Each removal causes Kabelo to delete one log-drum note from the next phrase. When only the original fifth remains, the drums stop completely. Seoyeon releases her breath before the final scheduled low note, so Kabelo withholds it. The recording ends with the faint mechanical return of a parcel scale in the room.

